+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Virtio-msg Linux Bridge Internals
+=================================
+
+This document describes implementation details of the QEMU
+``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` backend.
+
+For user-facing command-line usage, see:
+
+- :doc:`../system/devices/virtio/virtio-msg-linux-bridge`
+
+Scope
+-----
+
+The Linux bridge backend connects the ``virtio-msg`` transport core to the
+host kernel bridge UAPI (``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``) using:
+
+- control ioctls for endpoint lifecycle and topology
+- shared TX/RX rings for message traffic
+- eventfds for kick/call notifications
+
+QOM Model
+---------
+
+The backend supports two compositions:
+
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport`` (recommended for CLI use)
+  A plain ``TYPE_DEVICE`` container that creates an outer bus, a
+  ``virtio-msg`` proxy, and an internal ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge``
+  backend. The transport parent fixes default ring geometry to
+  256 entries with 128-byte slots (the aligned ``VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE``
+  default) and passes that value as ``vmm-max-msg-size``.
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` (standalone backend)
+  Attaches to an already existing ``virtio-msg`` object. Ring entry-size
+  validation is keyed to the backend's stored ``vmm-max-msg-size`` property.
+
+The backend itself derives from ``TYPE_VIRTIO_MSG_BUS_DEVICE`` and provides
+transport callbacks used by ``virtio-msg`` core.
+
+Realize and Attach Lifecycle
+----------------------------
+
+At realize time, the backend:
+
+1. Validates endpoint and ring geometry properties.
+2. Opens the bridge device node.
+3. Resolves endpoint string to a bridge endpoint handle
+   (``RESOLVE_F_CREATE`` only when ``create-endpoint=on``).
+4. Allocates shared ring backing and eventfds.
+5. Issues ``ATTACH`` with ring/eventfd descriptors plus the attach-epoch
+   ``vmm_max_msg_size`` input.
+6. Latches the attach snapshot (revision and feature bitmaps) and seeds
+   endpoint-scoped max-message state when the attach snapshot is online.
+7. Registers fd handlers for data-path and control-path processing.
+8. Installs a migration blocker.
+
+Unrealize performs the reverse sequence: unregister handlers, detach,
+release endpoint, unmap mapped areas, close fds, and remove the migration
+blocker.
+
+Endpoint State Model
+--------------------
+
+Endpoint availability is driven by an explicit attach-session state machine:
+
+- ``DETACHED``: no active bridge attach
+- ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE``: attach succeeded, but endpoint-scoped topology, map,
+  RX, and TX work is quiesced for the current epoch
+- ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``: the endpoint is online for the current
+  attach epoch, but transport traffic stays blocked until ``SET_DEVICES``
+  succeeds
+- ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``: topology has been published successfully for the
+  current online epoch, so RX, TX, map receive, map ACK, and pending-TX flush
+  are allowed
+- ``ATTACHED_FAILED``: reserved for terminal attach failure handling in later
+  steps
+
+State transitions for this step are:
+
+- ``ATTACH`` snapshot online -> ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- ``ATTACH`` snapshot offline -> ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE``
+- successful ``SET_DEVICES`` -> ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` -> ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` -> ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE``
+
+``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` is a lifecycle refresh for the current attach epoch: it
+updates endpoint-scoped fields (``bus_name`` and ``endpoint_max_msg_size``)
+and requests topology republish, but it is not a second attach-capability
+negotiation step.
+
+``CONTROL_RECV`` remains active in ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE`` and
+``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY`` so later lifecycle notifications can move
+the session forward again. Data and map traffic do not resume until the
+session reaches ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``.
+
+Control Path Contract
+---------------------
+
+Control traffic is processed from ``dev_fd`` using a non-blocking drain loop:
+
+- dequeue control message via ``CONTROL_RECV``
+- validate payload size and endpoint id
+- dispatch ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` and ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` by type
+
+``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` carries only endpoint lifecycle refresh data for the
+current attach epoch, including endpoint ``bus_name`` and endpoint
+``max_msg_size``.
+
+``EAGAIN``/``EWOULDBLOCK`` terminates the drain loop for the current wakeup.
+
+The simplified bridge UAPI has no ``CONTROL_ACK`` ioctl and no peer-state
+event family, so control-path ordering is limited to the delivered endpoint
+state transitions themselves.
+
+Data Path Contract
+------------------
+
+Data traffic uses shared TX/RX ring headers with monotonic ``u32`` cursors:
+
+- slot index: ``cursor % entries``
+- occupancy: ``prod - cons`` (unsigned arithmetic)
+- valid occupancy range: ``0..entries``
+
+Producer/consumer ordering follows acquire/release semantics:
+
+- producer publishes entry contents before advancing ``prod``
+- consumer reads ``prod`` with acquire semantics before reading payload
+- consumer advances ``cons`` only after processing is complete
+
+Occupancy above ``entries`` is treated as ring corruption and rejected.
+
+Copy-path behavior:
+
+- RX copies only the message header first, validates ``msg_size``, then copies
+  only validated payload bytes
+- TX copies only ``msg_size`` bytes to the ring slot (no full-slot clear)
+
+Ring Geometry and Validation
+----------------------------
+
+Bridge-backed ring policy is split between transport defaults and backend
+validation:
+
+- transport parent defaults are fixed to 256 entries and 128-byte slots for
+  both TX and RX
+- backend validation requires exactly 256 entries on both rings and slot size
+  ``== ALIGN(vmm_max_msg_size, 128)``
+- realize-time ring validation uses the backend's stored
+  ``vmm-max-msg-size`` property for both TX and RX
+- for the current v1 bridge UAPI this means transport-mode geometry is
+  effectively fixed to 256x128 rather than a generic runtime sizing knob
+- endpoint ``max_msg_size`` is learned per attach epoch from ``ATTACH`` and
+  later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` lifecycle refresh payloads
+
+Required Delivery Contract
+--------------------------
+
+Required traffic uses ``send_must_deliver`` semantics:
+
+- synchronous responses and used/config notifications are queued when transient
+  backpressure occurs
+- queued traffic is retried via a bounded timer-backed backoff only while the
+  session is in ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- existing bridge progress points also attempt to flush queued traffic when the
+  session returns to ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- retry is canceled when the queue drains or the session leaves the active
+  online state
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` clears queued pending-TX entries for the old epoch
+- no busy-loop retry path is used
+
+Queue Address Preparation
+-------------------------
+
+``SET_VQUEUE`` configures queue size, vector, and ring addresses.
+
+Descriptor payload mappings, indirect descriptor caches, and vring caches use
+the bridge ``dma_as`` backed by map-event-installed RAM subregions.
+
+Bridge-driven cache lifecycle:
+
+- queue region caches are invalidated after successful map add/del, endpoint
+  offline, and bridge teardown/reset paths
+- ``EVENT_AVAIL`` rebuilds queue caches only when invalid
+- coverage misses while offline fail fast without polling control/map progress
+
+DMA Mapping Model (Map Events + RAMBlock providers)
+---------------------------------------------------
+
+The backend map table is populated by the bridge map-event queue on ``dev_fd``:
+
+The final DMA-sharing path is entirely bridge-managed: map events publish and
+revoke generic DMA windows, and the transport does not use a ``GET_SHM``
+message to publish them.
+
+1. ``MAP_EVENT_RECV`` dequeues one event from ``dev_fd``.
+2. ``ADD`` validates the event, accepts ``mmap_length >= length``, maps
+   ``mmap_length`` bytes at ``mmap_offset`` from ``dev_fd``, and installs a
+   provider-backed RAM subregion into the bridge ``dma_mr`` at ``bus_addr``
+   for exactly ``length`` bytes.
+3. RAMBlock provider callbacks return direct host pointers from the bridge
+   entry, reverse-lookup host pointers back to the owning entry, and release
+   per-entry refs from ``address_space_unmap()`` and cache teardown.
+4. ``DEL_REQ`` revokes the DMA-visible subregion immediately, returns
+   ``MAP_ACK_RETRY`` while bridge-local references remain, and returns
+   ``MAP_ACK_OK`` only on a later retry after ``munmap()`` finalization
+   completes.
+5. ``MAP_EVENT_ACK`` is issued per dequeued event, with transient failures
+   retried on later ``dev_fd`` wakeups.
+
+Map entry identity and references are owned by bridge-local mapcache state:
+
+- host-pointer reverse lookup is local to the bridge backend provider callbacks
+- active direct-map and cache refs are tracked per map entry and block final
+  teardown
+- map add/del contracts require page-aligned exact spans and non-zero lengths
+  (zero bus addresses are accepted)
+
+Lookup optimization:
+
+- a single-entry covering-map cache is checked before QTAILQ scan
+- each hit/install updates ``last_use_seq`` through
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_touch_locked()``
+- cache is invalidated on entry removal, full unmap, and lifecycle resets
+
+Lifetime rules:
+
+- no opportunistic eviction is performed for cache pressure
+- map lifetime ends only on ``DEL_REQ``, endpoint offline, detach, or bridge
+  teardown
+- teardown removes any remaining live mappings and releases metadata
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` also clears any pending map-ACK state for the old epoch
+
+Capability Latching
+-------------------
+
+Transport capability selection is latched at child plug time:
+
+- extra features and transport capability snapshots are fixed during pre-plug
+- runtime control messages update endpoint state, but do not rewrite latched
+  transport capability model used by already plugged children
+
+Capability validation and max-size derivation are split by source:
+
+- ``ATTACH`` validates ``revision`` against
+  ``VIRTIO_MSG_LINUX_BRIDGE_REVISION_SUPPORTED``
+- ``max_msg_size`` normalization: ``0 -> VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE``
+- bounds: ``virtio_msg_header_size() <= max_msg_size <= sizeof(VirtIOMSG)``
+- attach snapshots provide the only source for transport and bridge feature
+  bitmaps
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` refreshes endpoint ``bus_name`` and endpoint
+  ``max_msg_size`` only (no revision/features update)
+- effective transport max is recomputed as
+  ``min(vmm_max_msg_size, endpoint_max_msg_size_when_online)``, with the
+  endpoint term absent while offline
+
+Topology Publication Model
+--------------------------
+
+Topology publication uses a full-snapshot ``SET_DEVICES`` model:
+
+- attach-online snapshots and later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` transitions enter
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- ``machine_done`` and ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` may publish topology only from
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- successful ``SET_DEVICES`` is the only transition into
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- failed ``SET_DEVICES`` leaves the session attached but quiesced in
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY`` until a later successful publish
+- transient ``SET_DEVICES`` failures (for example ``EAGAIN``/``EBUSY``) use a
+  backend-local bounded timer/backoff retry while the session remains in
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- this topology retry is canceled once ``SET_DEVICES`` succeeds or the session
+  leaves ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- the published range is ``first_dev_num = 0`` through
+  ``last_dev_num = VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_DEVS - 1``
+- bitmap bit ``n`` maps to external device number ``first_dev_num + n``
+- runtime child hotplug and unplug are unsupported
+- no runtime ``DEV_NUM_ADD``/``DEV_NUM_REMOVE`` publication path is kept
+- the ``virtio-msg`` proxy bus is intentionally not hotpluggable
+
+``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is treated as an attached-but-offline epoch transition,
+not as an implicit detach. QEMU keeps the bridge session attached, clears
+pending-TX entries and retry state, clears pending map-ACK state, unmaps all
+bridge mappings, invalidates proxy caches, and resets attached child devices.
+The device reset is retained QEMU transport policy layered on top of the Linux
+bridge ABI rather than an ABI requirement.
+
+Concurrency Notes
+-----------------
+
+The backend separates wakeup handling by fd role:
+
+- ``call_fd`` callback runs control/map processing before RX ring dequeue,
+  allowing endpoint-state and map-event progress on RX wakeups
+- ``dev_fd`` callback drains both control and map-event queues, and retries
+  pending map-event ACKs as needed (primary wakeup source)
+
+This split keeps RX ring traffic isolated while preserving ordered handling for
+endpoint-state and map-event transitions, even when control work is triggered
+by ``call_fd`` wakeups.
+
+Migration
+---------
+
+Migration is explicitly blocked for this backend.
+
+Reason: bridge session state (resolved endpoint handle, attached fd state,
+map tokens, and host bridge lifecycle) is host-local and cannot be
+transparently reconstructed on destination.
+
+Tracing and Debugging
+---------------------
+
+Relevant diagnostics include:
+
+- guest-error logs for malformed control payloads or endpoint mismatch
+- warning logs for short/failed eventfd writes and fd read errors
+- tracepoints covering endpoint and peer state transitions
+- map lifecycle traces:
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_recv``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_ack``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_install``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_remove``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_busy``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_evict``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_ref_acquire``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_ref_release``
